257
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:12,680
or anything else.

258
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:14,060
But, money's money.

259
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:15,160
Right? No problem.

260
00:11:15,160 --> 00:11:15,880
Gains to trade.

261
00:11:15,880 --> 00:11:17,320
Like that's perfectly fine with him.

262
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:20,960
As long as it's actually a practical task.

263
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:24,120
But when it comes to any kind of a policy question

264
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:27,520
or analyzing society, that's where the heuristic

265
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:31,520
of who do I have antipathy for is basically infallible for him.

266
00:11:34,260 --> 00:11:35,620
So, what I say is, you know,

267
00:11:35,620 --> 00:11:38,400
even smart people become extremely stupid

268
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:40,880
in political matters because the costs are so low.

269
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:43,560
And you can see how many pundits,

270
00:11:43,560 --> 00:11:45,920
generally very smart people, open up their mouths

271
00:11:45,920 --> 00:11:47,240
and say ridiculous things.

272
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:50,740
And then when challenged to bet on it, either run for the hills,

273
00:11:51,280 --> 00:11:53,200
say ah, or ignore you.

274
00:11:53,380 --> 00:11:56,400
Or just go and change the subject or say, well,

275
00:11:56,400 --> 00:11:58,200
I'll bet on something that is totally different

276
00:11:58,200 --> 00:11:59,120
from what I originally said.

277
00:12:00,380 --> 00:12:01,260
Now, yet occasionally they'll say,

278
00:12:01,260 --> 00:12:02,600
well why should I give you odds?

279
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:03,320
Well, why should I?

280
00:12:03,320 --> 00:12:04,960
I'd like to get good odds for my bets.

281
00:12:04,960 --> 00:12:07,640
You should give me good odds for my bet

282
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,960
because you claim high confidence and I don't.

283
00:12:11,320 --> 00:12:14,200
So, you should give me good odds intermediate between even

284
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:16,900
and yours to entice me to bet in order to sucker me in.

285
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:18,140
That's why.

286
00:12:19,460 --> 00:12:22,440
It's the logic of trade in general.

287
00:12:22,820 --> 00:12:25,300
Right? If I can produce something for one-fifth

288
00:12:25,300 --> 00:12:27,900
of the cost that you can produce it for, I don't go and try

289
00:12:27,900 --> 00:12:30,260
to charge you the same price that you can make it

290
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:33,140
for yourself, I offer you a price good enough to get you

291
00:12:33,140 --> 00:12:33,780
to go and trade with me.

292
00:12:33,780 --> 00:12:36,140
Right? And it's exactly the same.

293
00:12:36,140 --> 00:12:39,700
If I say, you know, if I say 50-50, you say 90%,

294
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:42,120
then you should be happy to give me two to one odds in order

295
00:12:42,120 --> 00:12:43,460
to induce me to bet with you.

296
00:12:44,160 --> 00:12:46,920
And if you don't want to give odds like that, that is a sign

297
00:12:46,920 --> 00:12:48,000
that you don't have the confidence

298
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:48,960
that we are planning to have.

299
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:53,360
So, what I say is voter rationality is, first of all,

300
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:55,480
plausible in theory because of this.

301
00:12:56,540 --> 00:12:59,340
You know, democracy is exactly the kind of institutional setting

302
00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:01,120
where people can be completely crazy

303
00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:03,360
without any noticeable effect on themselves.

304
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:04,880
And first of all, and second of all,

305
00:13:04,880 --> 00:13:07,160
it's also easy to detect empirically on a large scale.

306
00:13:07,840 --> 00:13:10,200
In fact, our stereotypes that economists have

307
00:13:10,200 --> 00:13:13,260
about public opinion and public beliefs about economics,

308
00:13:13,600 --> 00:13:15,640
almost all are confirmed by systematic data.

309
00:13:17,180 --> 00:13:19,960
And if you take public choice class,

310
00:13:19,960 --> 00:13:22,280
you'll go over a bunch of other stereotypes

311
00:13:22,280 --> 00:13:24,300
about how people are clueless and how right those are.

312
00:13:25,040 --> 00:13:29,240
One fun one is there is a stereotype that there's a lot

313
00:13:29,240 --> 00:13:31,800
of that all you need to do

314
00:13:31,800 --> 00:13:33,700
to make a war popular is just declare war

315
00:13:33,700 --> 00:13:35,540
and then people rally around and wave flags.

316
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:38,200
And this is very strongly borne out by the data.

317
00:13:38,920 --> 00:13:40,400
It's called the rally around the flag effect.

318
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:41,920
If you just take a look at public opinion,

319
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:45,680
even if the last poll before war is declared makes the war

320
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:48,840
unpopular, just declaring it causes a big spike

321
00:13:48,840 --> 00:13:50,960
in popularity and then makes it popular.

322
00:13:52,560 --> 00:13:55,320
So, you know, like emotionally this makes perfect sense, right?

323
00:13:55,800 --> 00:13:57,460
Because you hear your country's at war

324
00:13:57,460 --> 00:13:59,720
and what is a normal human being's emotional reaction

325
00:13:59,720 --> 00:14:00,040
to that?

326
00:14:01,820 --> 00:14:03,800
USA, USA, right?

327
00:14:03,800 --> 00:14:05,140
Well, Americans' reaction.

328
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:08,040
Like say, whatever country we're in, Guatemala.

329
00:14:08,040 --> 00:14:12,540
All right, so these are normal human reactions.

330
00:14:12,540 --> 00:14:15,980
You hear your country's at war and you want to support them

331
00:14:15,980 --> 00:14:18,360
and part of supporting them is saying it's a great idea

332
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:19,400
and that we're going to win.

333
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:21,820
Right? You've probably heard about the beginning

334
00:14:21,820 --> 00:14:22,520
of World War I.

335
00:14:22,520 --> 00:14:24,380
There are parades in all the big countries.

336
00:14:24,380 --> 00:14:27,040
Happy parades of imminent victory.

337
00:14:28,040 --> 00:14:28,760
They're all wrong.

338
00:14:29,320 --> 00:14:31,520
Right? Now, you might say, well, maybe some people thought

339
00:14:31,520 --> 00:14:32,860
that they were really likely to win.

340
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:34,840
Yeah, but everyone seems to think they were likely to win.

341
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:36,800
And that doesn't make sense.

342
00:14:37,300 --> 00:14:38,660
You know, if you're in France and you say, well,

343
00:14:39,040 --> 00:14:39,820
why are we so happy?

344
00:14:39,820 --> 00:14:41,200
The Germans are having a really big parade.

345
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:42,300
Maybe they didn't beat us last time

346
00:14:42,300 --> 00:14:43,200
and they're going to beat us again.

347
00:14:43,900 --> 00:14:46,280
Right? That is not, that is the kind of thing

348
00:14:46,280 --> 00:14:48,480
that is going to make enemies and it's also the kind

349
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:51,480
of thing that makes you feel like a traitor, even to think.

350
00:14:51,480 --> 00:14:54,540
Right? You know, just the thought of our country can lose.

351
00:14:55,360 --> 00:14:57,460
Those people, to think things like this normally.

352
00:14:58,060 --> 00:15:00,480
And things that hurt you to believe are things

353
00:15:00,480 --> 00:15:01,960
that people tend not to believe.

354
00:15:03,860 --> 00:15:07,660
Right? Now, Whitman is always demanding, if you read him,

355
00:15:08,220 --> 00:15:10,100
for, he's demanding those critics say,

356
00:15:10,100 --> 00:15:13,360
what is the key asymmetry between markets and politics?

357
00:15:13,640 --> 00:15:14,800
How are they really different?

358
00:15:14,800 --> 00:15:18,160
Isn't it all just a competitive interaction

359
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:21,140
where people are trying to please customers or voters

360
00:15:21,140 --> 00:15:24,020
and why don't, why wouldn't both systems work equally well,

361
00:15:24,020 --> 00:15:25,020
at least within their sphere?

362
00:15:25,740 --> 00:15:29,940
And my answer is the key asymmetry is incentives

363
00:15:29,940 --> 00:15:30,560
for rationality.

364
00:15:31,740 --> 00:15:34,340
So, in markets, there are plenty of people

365
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:35,940
who I will say are still irrational.

366
00:15:36,260 --> 00:15:39,540
But at least if you're rational, you get something out of it.

367
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:41,180
You can avoid harm to yourself.

368
00:15:41,580 --> 00:15:42,600
You can get a better deal.

369
00:15:43,480 --> 00:15:45,260
Right? Whereas in politics,

370
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:48,820
if you are the lone rational person, what happens to you?

371
00:15:48,820 --> 00:15:51,640
Same thing that happens to everybody else.

372
00:15:51,640 --> 00:15:54,380
Right? So, in markets, even when people are ignorant,

373
00:15:54,380 --> 00:15:56,100
they generally try to apply common sense,

374
00:15:56,100 --> 00:15:58,340
which is why internet scams generally don't work.

375
00:15:59,480 --> 00:16:03,320
Right? So, you know, you may not know a lot of details

376
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:07,560
about Nigeria or why there's so many spam emails from them.

377
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:09,540
But you do have common sense.

378
00:16:09,540 --> 00:16:11,280
And common sense tells you to delete those people.

379
00:16:12,200 --> 00:16:14,100
Right? And what are some common sense sayings

380
00:16:14,100 --> 00:16:16,740
that would advise you to go and just delete them?

381
00:16:16,740 --> 00:16:22,780
How about if it sounds too good to be true, it probably is.

382
00:16:23,320 --> 00:16:24,320
That's just common sense.

383
00:16:24,800 --> 00:16:26,720
Doesn't require much information to apply it.

384
00:16:26,720 --> 00:16:27,840
It's just like, who are you?

385
00:16:28,140 --> 00:16:28,720
I don't know you.

386
00:16:29,260 --> 00:16:30,720
You're offering me something that sounds really great.

387
00:16:31,100 --> 00:16:31,700
I don't believe it.

388
00:16:31,920 --> 00:16:33,100
Delete. Right?

389
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:34,060
People do this all the time.

390
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:38,260
But politicians do the same kind of thing, and it works.

391
00:16:38,720 --> 00:16:41,520
Politician comes along and says, this is going to be fantastic.

392
00:16:41,520 --> 00:16:42,960
I'm going to give you this great deal.

393
00:16:43,260 --> 00:16:46,000
Like, we're going to, like, everyone will be insured.

394
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:47,320
The costs will go down.

395
00:16:47,320 --> 00:16:48,600
It's going to be wonderful.

396
00:16:49,040 --> 00:16:50,320
Right? And people believe them.

397
00:16:50,740 --> 00:16:53,940
Or someone says, look, we're going to go and invade Iraq.

398
00:16:54,300 --> 00:16:55,980
We're going to put a stall on democracy.

399
00:16:56,200 --> 00:16:57,700
And we're going to bring peace to the Middle East.

400
00:16:57,780 --> 00:16:59,160
And it's going to happen in our time.

401
00:16:59,160 --> 00:16:59,900
And we're going to do it.

402
00:16:59,900 --> 00:17:00,840
And you get to be part of it.

403
00:17:01,240 --> 00:17:03,000
That whole story, that sounds like a great story.

404
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:03,840
It's going to be cheap.

405
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:05,000
Cheap, fast.

406
00:17:05,560 --> 00:17:06,280
Right? Or let's see.

407
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:08,720
Last night, I was seeing a clip

408
00:17:08,820 --> 00:17:11,900
from John F. Kennedy's Cuban Missile Crisis speech.

409
00:17:11,900 --> 00:17:16,060
And one line, I think it's like, this is almost word for word.

410
00:17:16,060 --> 00:17:17,480
He said, you know, the greatest risk

411
00:17:17,480 --> 00:17:18,520
of all would be to do nothing.

412
00:17:19,900 --> 00:17:20,160
That's it.

413
00:17:20,660 --> 00:17:21,740
How do you know?

414
00:17:21,740 --> 00:17:23,300
I mean, this never happened before.

415
00:17:23,300 --> 00:17:26,060
It seems like there's a really, there's one scenario

416
00:17:26,060 --> 00:17:27,940
where this causes a nuclear war,

417
00:17:27,940 --> 00:17:29,560
which we could have avoided just by appeasement.

418
00:17:30,400 --> 00:17:32,740
Maybe it'll be even worse if we don't fight now.

419
00:17:32,740 --> 00:17:34,120
But then again, maybe not.

420
00:17:34,660 --> 00:17:35,460
How do you really know?

421
00:17:36,120 --> 00:17:38,240
What, like, where's the, well, like, can we have a bet on this?

422
00:17:38,240 --> 00:17:40,700
And, you know, we, but, you know, none of that is there.

423
00:17:40,700 --> 00:17:41,900
And Stan, he just says that.

424
00:17:42,260 --> 00:17:44,660
And again, when, and of course, he says stuff like this

425
00:17:44,660 --> 00:17:45,700
because he knows, you know,

426
00:17:45,700 --> 00:17:47,740
as a very successful politician, this appeals to people.

427
00:17:48,280 --> 00:17:51,460
They want, you know, a forceful leader who tells them,

428
00:17:51,460 --> 00:17:52,820
like, we are going to act.

429
00:17:53,120 --> 00:17:54,600
We are going to solve the problem.

430
00:17:54,960 --> 00:17:58,600
And those naysayers who just want to leave well enough alone

431
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:02,240
are going to make the whole, can only make the problem worse.

432
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:06,180
Not just to say might or seems likely to be, you know,

433
00:18:06,180 --> 00:18:08,000
the kind of thing that would be the honest thing to say.

434
00:18:08,700 --> 00:18:09,820
Right? Say, well, you know, and again,

435
00:18:09,820 --> 00:18:12,980
just imagine a president saying, look, I've crunched the numbers.

436
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:16,200
There's a 40% chance my action improves things.

437
00:18:16,620 --> 00:18:18,300
40% chance it leaves them the same.

438
00:18:18,300 --> 00:18:20,100
20% chance that it makes it worse.

439
00:18:20,520 --> 00:18:21,680
It's a favorable bet.

440
00:18:22,220 --> 00:18:22,860
So let's do it.

441
00:18:23,780 --> 00:18:25,120
That is not a war president.

442
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:27,920
That is not someone who is rallying a country behind him

443
00:18:27,920 --> 00:18:28,520
for anything.

444
00:18:29,360 --> 00:18:31,580
Instead, people like a leader just says, look,

445
00:18:32,060 --> 00:18:34,020
victory is ours if we believe it.

446
00:18:36,120 --> 00:18:39,800
Dot. Like, okay, like, is that generally true?

447
00:18:39,800 --> 00:18:41,020
Is that only our country?

448
00:18:41,020 --> 00:18:42,960
Does every country get this amazing,

449
00:18:43,340 --> 00:18:46,660
this amazing Tinkerbell principle that if we just believe

450
00:18:46,660 --> 00:18:48,080
in fairies that it happens?

451
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:49,040
No. Wrong.

452
00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:53,860
But, like, why would politicians say this ridiculous nonsense?

453
00:18:53,860 --> 00:18:54,880
And not just one person.

454
00:18:54,880 --> 00:18:55,520
They all do it.

455
00:18:56,040 --> 00:18:57,980
Why do they all say this ridiculous nonsense?

456
00:18:59,380 --> 00:19:01,400
Right? You know, like, just a bunch of literal,

457
00:19:01,400 --> 00:19:03,520
one literal falsehood after another.

458
00:19:04,500 --> 00:19:06,620
Right? And people believe this stuff.

459
00:19:06,660 --> 00:19:07,340
People like it.

460
00:19:07,340 --> 00:19:10,200
You know, maybe part of it is people are just, well,

461
00:19:10,200 --> 00:19:11,260
like, just like poetry.

462
00:19:11,900 --> 00:19:13,840
But, again, I've talked to enough people

463
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:16,900
who tell me the poems with very, with great seriousness

464
00:19:16,900 --> 00:19:18,280
as if they've got it all figured out.

465
00:19:19,040 --> 00:19:20,820
I think that they actually believe the poems.

466
00:19:21,500 --> 00:19:24,180
Like, you know, it's, someone believes a poem is

467
00:19:24,180 --> 00:19:24,920
literally true.

468
00:19:24,920 --> 00:19:26,960
That's what it looks like to me.

469
00:19:26,960 --> 00:19:29,940
Like, every time someone says, this can only result in this.

470
00:19:30,740 --> 00:19:32,660
So you'll bet me on, like, a million to one odds.

471
00:19:32,660 --> 00:19:34,600
Or, no, even odds.

472
00:19:34,600 --> 00:19:35,280
Or no bet at all.

473
00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:39,280
Wow. One plus one can only equal two.

474
00:19:39,280 --> 00:19:40,820
Now, that I'll bet a lot of money on.

475
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:42,920
But they don't want to bet a lot of money

476
00:19:42,920 --> 00:19:45,260
on all the things they claim can only be a certain way.

477
00:19:46,260 --> 00:19:46,440
All right.

478
00:19:46,940 --> 00:19:50,700
Now, last thing that we get out of my rationality story is

479
00:19:50,700 --> 00:19:52,760
it explains why a successful politician doesn't try

480
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:53,640
to fix the voters.

481
00:19:54,460 --> 00:19:56,160
Successful politician isn't out to educate.

482
00:19:56,680 --> 00:19:58,360
Successful politician is out to pander.

483
00:19:58,360 --> 00:20:05,360
To tell people what they want to hear.


